 Deep Models for Semantic Compositionality Over a Sentiment Treebank","url":"https://preview.aclanthology.org/setup/D13-1170"},{"title":"Recursive Deep Models for Semantic Compositionality Over a Sentiment Treebank","url":"https://preview.aclanthology.org/teach-a-man-to-fish/D13-1170"}]},"The paper is by Richard Socher, Alex Perelygin, Jean Wu, Jason Chuang, Christopher D. Manning, Andrew Ng, and Christopher Potts. It was published at EMNLP 2013, the Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing, in Seattle, with pages 1631–1642 (Anthology ID D13-1170). The ACL Anthology lists it under the Association for Computational Linguistics.\n\nTwo other details came up. One source says the paper received the ACL 10-Year Test of Time Paper Award. Another lists it as the Most Influential EMNLP 2013 Paper, based on a third-party ranking. I'd treat the award as the more reliable of the two, since it comes from the ACL Anthology record."],"durationSeconds":4.742503514000011,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01WFnZUSgwKvXkFPhrkkk7qu","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TyYxT3Pv9XVGasamN8ntBo","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Michel Levy Neubig \"Are Sixteen Heads Really Better than One?\" NeurIPS 2019 up to 60% heads pruned\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Are Sixteen Heads Really Better than One?\",\"url\":\"https://papers.nips.cc/paper/2019/hash/2c601ad9d2ff9bc8b282670cdd54f69f-Abstract.html\"},{\"title\":\"Are Sixteen Heads Really Better than One?\",\"url\":\"https://papers.nips.cc/paper/9551-are-sixteen-heads-really-better-than-one\"},{\"title\":\"Are Sixteen Heads Really Better than One?\",\"url\":\"https://nips.cc/virtual/2019/poster/14330\"},{\"title\":\"neurips.cc\",\"url\":\"https://neurips.cc/virtual/2019/poster/14330\"},{\"title\":\"arxiv.org\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.10650v2\"},{\"title\":\"alphaxiv.org\",\"url\":\"https://alphaxiv.org/overview/1905.10650v3\"},{\"title\":\"Are Sixteen Heads Really Better than One?\",\"url\":\"https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/1905.10650\"},{\"title\":\"Are Sixteen Heads Really Better than One?\",\"url\":\"https://mlanthology.org/neurips/2019/michel2019neurips-sixteen\"},{\"title\":\"Are Sixteen Heads Really Better than One?\",\"url\":\"https://sotaverified.org/papers/are-sixteen-heads-really-better-than-one\"},{\"title\":\"Paper · arXiv:1905.10650 · 25 May 2019\",\"url\":\"https://paperswithcode.co/paper/1905.10650\"}]\n\nThe paper is by Paul Michel, Omer Levy, and Graham Neubig, published at NeurIPS 2019 (Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems 32). Your author list is correct, though the first author is Paul Michel.\n\n- **Core finding:** Attention heads can be removed at test time with little loss. The authors found that some layers can even be cut down to a single head, and that the encoder-decoder layers in machine translation models depend more on multiple heads than self-attention layers do. (a large proportion of attention heads can be removed at test time without significantly impacting performance, and that some layers can even be reduced to a single head. Further analysis on machine translation models reveals that the self-attention layers can be significantly pruned, while the encoder-decoder layers are more dependent on multi-headedness.)\n- **Method:** Rather than retraining, they mask heads at test time by zeroing their outputs (individual heads are \"turned off\" at test time by setting their outputs to zero).\n- **The 60% figure:** I could only confirm this through a third-party summary, not the paper's own text. That summary says self-attention layers maintain reasonable performance even when 60-70% of heads are pruned. I didn't find the paper stating \"up to 60%\" directly, so verify the exact figure against the PDF before citing it.\n- **BERT speedup:** The summary also reports that when attention heads are actually removed from BERT models (rather than just masked), inference speed increases substantially - up to 17.5% speedup for larger batch sizes when 50% of heads are pruned.\n\nSources: the NeurIPS proceedings page (papers.nips.cc), the arXiv listing (1905.10650), and the alphaXiv overview (for the 60–70% and speedup figures).\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3f42d925-3fb0-401c-b4de-561355f745c0","uuid":"7c8b1e9f-d85c-4c4d-b2ef-378fbb669ad7","timestamp":"2026-10-31T07:25:49.635Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"Michel Levy Neubig \"Are Sixteen Heads Really Better than One?\" NeurIPS 2019 up to 60% heads pruned","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01Q9Y24CcXcrL9MFnrSJzAhx","content":[{"title":"Are Sixteen Heads Really Better than One?","url":"https://papers.nips.cc/paper/2019/hash/2c601ad9d2ff9bc8b282670cdd54f69f-Abstract.html"},{"title":"Are Sixteen Heads Really Better than One?","url":"https://papers.nips.cc/paper/9551-are-sixteen-heads-really-better-than-one"},{"title":"Are Sixteen Heads Really Better than One?","url":"https://nips.cc/virtual/2019/poster/14330"},{"title":"neurips.cc","url":"https://neurips.cc/virtual/2019/poster/14330"},{"title":"arxiv.org","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.10650v2"},{"title":"alphaxiv.org","url":"https://alphaxiv.org/overview/1905.10650v3"},{"title":"Are Sixteen Heads Really Better than One?","url":"https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/1905.10650"},{"title":"Are Sixteen Heads Really Better than One?","url":"https://mlanthology.org/neurips/2019/michel2019neurips-sixteen"},{"title":"Are Sixteen Heads Really Better than One?","url":"https://sotaverified.org/papers/are-sixteen-heads-really-better-than-one"},{"title":"Paper · arXiv:1905.10650 · 25 May 2019","url":"https://paperswithcode.co/paper/1905.10650"}]},"The paper is by Paul Michel, Omer Levy, and Graham Neubig, published at NeurIPS 2019 (Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems 32). Your author list is correct, though the first author is Paul Michel.\n\n- **Core finding:** Attention heads can be removed at test time with little loss. The authors found that some layers can even be cut down to a single head, and that the encoder-decoder layers in machine translation models depend more on multiple heads than self-attention layers do. (a large proportion of attention heads can be removed at test time without significantly impacting performance, and that some layers can even be reduced to a single head. Further analysis on machine translation models reveals that the self-attention layers can be significantly pruned, while the encoder-decoder layers are more dependent on multi-headedness.)\n- **Method:** Rather than retraining, they mask heads at test time by zeroing their outputs (individual heads are \"turned off\" at test time by setting their outputs to zero).\n- **The 60% figure:** I could only confirm this through a third-party summary, not the paper's own text. That summary says self-attention layers maintain reasonable performance even when 60-70% of heads are pruned. I didn't find the paper stating \"up to 60%\" directly, so verify the exact figure against the PDF before citing it.\n- **BERT speedup:** The summary also reports that when attention heads are actually removed from BERT models (rather than just masked), inference speed increases substantially - up to 17.5% speedup for larger batch sizes when 50% of heads are pruned.\n\nSources: the NeurIPS proceedings page (papers.nips.cc), the arXiv listing (1905.10650), and the alphaXiv overview (for the 60–70% and speedup figures)."],"durationSeconds":7.254451113999996,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01TyYxT3Pv9XVGasamN8ntBo","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
