{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01R5rLRriHtPvZxtywQcN4ds","type":"tool_result","content":"The paper describes the spectrum of the self-attention context mapping matrix *P* in pretrained RoBERTa models, not a matrix from a model trained in the paper's setup.\n\n- On the long tail: \"The results exhibit a clear long-tail spectrum distribution across each layer, head and task.\"\n- On higher layers: \"We observe that the spectrum distribution in higher layers is more skewed than in lower layers,\"\n- On what that means: \"meaning that, in higher layers, more information is concentrated in the largest singular values\""}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3f42d925-3fb0-401c-b4de-561355f745c0","uuid":"246ad91c-807f-478d-a7ce-f3744cc9818e","timestamp":"2026-10-31T07:26:10.206Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":242009,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"The paper describes the spectrum of the self-attention context mapping matrix *P* in pretrained RoBERTa models, not a matrix from a model trained in the paper's setup.\n\n- On the long tail: \"The results exhibit a clear long-tail spectrum distribution across each layer, head and task.\"\n- On higher layers: \"We observe that the spectrum distribution in higher layers is more skewed than in lower layers,\"\n- On what that means: \"meaning that, in higher layers, more information is concentrated in the largest singular values\"","durationMs":4383,"url":"https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2006.04768"},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01R5rLRriHtPvZxtywQcN4ds","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WEgY8VipqgJSQKUe2D4CQ8","type":"tool_result","content":"**How much can be pruned (per the paper):**\n\n- **WMT (translation) and BERT (MNLI):** The paper states that the approach allows pruning \"up to 20% and 40% of heads from WMT and BERT (respectively)\" (Section 4.2).\n- **Additional BERT tasks (Appendix B):** \"up to 60% (SST-2) or 50% (CoLA, MRPC) of heads can be pruned\" without a noticeable performance impact. These are fine-tuned BERT models.\n- **Encoder-decoder attention (WMT, Section 5):** Pruning \"more than 60% of the Enc-Dec attention heads will result in catastrophic performance degradation.\" Encoder and decoder self-attention stayed at reasonable BLEU with only 20% of heads kept.\n\n**How importance is ranked:**\n\n- Heads are sorted by a proxy score, the expected absolute sensitivity of the loss to each head's mask variable. The paper says this equals the expected magnitude of the head's output times the loss gradient with respect to that output.\n- The score is averaged over training data (or a subset) and requires only one forward and backward pass. Scores are normalized within each layer using the ℓ2 norm.\n- Pruning proceeds iteratively, removing the lowest-scoring heads in steps of 10% of all heads. The paper says that the sorting method \"sort all the attention heads in the model according to a proxy importance score\" is faster and yields better results than ranking by the single-head ablation score difference."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3f42d925-3fb0-401c-b4de-561355f745c0","uuid":"21f10734-61b0-4964-8bac-d6eac3be874a","timestamp":"2026-10-31T07:26:14.156Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":171379,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"**How much can be pruned (per the paper):**\n\n- **WMT (translation) and BERT (MNLI):** The paper states that the approach allows pruning \"up to 20% and 40% of heads from WMT and BERT (respectively)\" (Section 4.2).\n- **Additional BERT tasks (Appendix B):** \"up to 60% (SST-2) or 50% (CoLA, MRPC) of heads can be pruned\" without a noticeable performance impact. These are fine-tuned BERT models.\n- **Encoder-decoder attention (WMT, Section 5):** Pruning \"more than 60% of the Enc-Dec attention heads will result in catastrophic performance degradation.\" Encoder and decoder self-attention stayed at reasonable BLEU with only 20% of heads kept.\n\n**How importance is ranked:**\n\n- Heads are sorted by a proxy score, the expected absolute sensitivity of the loss to each head's mask variable. The paper says this equals the expected magnitude of the head's output times the loss gradient with respect to that output.\n- The score is averaged over training data (or a subset) and requires only one forward and backward pass. Scores are normalized within each layer using the ℓ2 norm.\n- Pruning proceeds iteratively, removing the lowest-scoring heads in steps of 10% of all heads. The paper says that the sorting method \"sort all the attention heads in the model according to a proxy importance score\" is faster and yields better results than ranking by the single-head ablation score difference.","durationMs":10433,"url":"https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/1905.10650"},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01WEgY8VipqgJSQKUe2D4CQ8","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
