{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014kdqsedmE5nfZeF4pXsgBA","type":"tool_result","content":"    Initialization Sets the Direction of Attention Rank\n                          Change:\n     Testing Five Claims About Spectral Dynamics in\n                          Training\n\n\n                                               NeuriCo\n\n\n\n                                              Abstract\n\n         Attention matrices are often said to collapse in rank during training, and spec-\n         tral statistics are attractive as cheap guides for pruning. Most evidence, how-\n         ever, comes from finished models. We test five specific claims: heads split into\n         high- and low-rank populations, entropy follows layer-specific shapes, heads     √be-\n         low 0.3 log n entropy can be pruned safely, I(A; X) ≤ H(Y |X) + O(1/ n),\n         and the last layer reaches its rank minimum three to five times faster than the first.\n         We train 68 transformers across 13 configurations on text, vision, and pretrained\n         BERT, tracking every head at 41 checkpoints, and run 120 further models with\n         controlled H(Y |X). No run meets the bifurcation criterion under any of five rank\n         definitions. Under default initialization attention starts nearly uniform and rank\n6","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3f42d925-3fb0-401c-b4de-561355f745c0","uuid":"573196c2-4785-449d-a70b-7fab983086ff","timestamp":"2026-10-31T07:30:02.534Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"    Initialization Sets the Direction of Attention Rank\n                          Change:\n     Testing Five Claims About Spectral Dynamics in\n                          Training\n\n\n                                               NeuriCo\n\n\n\n                                              Abstract\n\n         Attention matrices are often said to collapse in rank during training, and spec-\n         tral statistics are attractive as cheap guides for pruning. Most evidence, how-\n         ever, comes from finished models. We test five specific claims: heads split into\n         high- and low-rank populations, entropy follows layer-specific shapes, heads     √be-\n         low 0.3 log n entropy can be pruned safely, I(A; X) ≤ H(Y |X) + O(1/ n),\n         and the last layer reaches its rank minimum three to five times faster than the first.\n         We train 68 transformers across 13 configurations on text, vision, and pretrained\n         BERT, tracking every head at 41 checkpoints, and run 120 further models with\n         controlled H(Y |X). No run meets the bifurcation criterion under any of five rank\n         definitions. Under default initialization attention starts nearly uniform and rank\n6","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_014kdqsedmE5nfZeF4pXsgBA","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
